stephen king disliked the movie shelley  duvall is traumatized by it the studio  went up in flames there's more stoking  the fire behind the many untold truths  of the shining masterpiece curious about  what's happening behind the overlook  hotel doors  after watching stanley kubrick's creepy  adaptation of the shining it's probably  hard for most to imagine anyone other  than jack nicholson in the role of jack  torrance however the legendary actor was  far from the only choice for the role in  his book fright favorites david j skal  says that stephen king rejected several  options that kubrick proposed for the  lead king was more of a fan of having  someone like jon voight or michael  moriarty play the role instead king  eventually accepted nicholson as jack  torrance although he wound up regretting  that decision later why you may ask it's  primarily because he criticized how  kubrick wanted the character portrayed  in an interview with deadline king  explains the character of jack torrance  has no arc in that movie absolutely no  arc at all all he does is get crazier in  the book he's a guy who's struggling  with his sanity and finally loses it to  me that's a tragedy  stephen king not only disliked how jack  torrance was portrayed in the film but  was actually even more upset with wendy  torrence in the adaptation in an  interview with the bbc he claimed that  their portrayal was misogynistic adding  she's basically just there to scream and  be stupid and that's not the woman that  i wrote about  according to the mirror the author  wanted the character to be shown as a  strong woman that he initially wrote he  even envisioned jessica lan just someone  who could do so in the end kubrick went  in a completely different direction and  dramatically changed the character he  decided to go with representing someone  more anxious and fragile and felt that  shelley duvall was a better fit for the  part  kubrick's vision of wendy torrence  illustrates a woman who is in such an  abusive relationship that she's too  frightened to leave which is  unfortunately a difficult reality that  many people face the lens that kubrick  was willing to go to get his desired  performance out of shelley duvall was  also disturbing it ultimately made  filming quite a harrowing experience for  the 28 year old actress that would have  significant long-term effects on her i  don't sympathize with shelley  it doesn't help you wouldn't it yes it  does  in an interview with dr phil many years  later it's visible that duvall is still  traumatized by the year she spent on  sets she talked about the difficulty she  went through with filming before sadly  confessing if he hadn't directed the way  he did if he hadn't done everything with  force and cruelty then i guess it  wouldn't have turned out to be as it was  not only was the casting crew ordered to  ignore and isolate duvall but kubrick  also punished her as much as possible  one of the worst examples of this is  when she was forced to do 120  takes of the scene where she holds the  baseball bat as jack nicholson slowly  stalks towards her hours of crying on  camera left her dehydrated and hoarse  and her hands even bled from holding the  bat so tightly and for long kubrick's  daughter vivian shot behind the scenes  footage for making the shining which  features various moments of tension  between duval and kubrick it even showed  duvall's hair falling out due to the  unbearable stress of filming  one of the key features shared by both  the novel and the film is the eerie  atmosphere of the overlook hotel but do  you know what inspired it it's actually  based upon the stanley hotel in estes  colorado in late september 1974 stephen  king and his wife spent time at the  stanley hotel when it was nearly empty  as a resort was about to close for  winter the silent holes and spooky  ambience creeped the author out so much  that it led to a nightmare that will  become the origin for the shining the  hotel has a long history of being  haunted in fact according to the new  york times the owners and staff have  leaned into the spooky image of the  stanley and used it as a marketing  strategy they've even gone as far as  adding the hedge maze kubrick created  for his film in 2015.  filming the shining may not have been as  hard for the rest of the cast and crew  as it was for duvall but it was still  extremely difficult a number of takes  kubrick wanted to shoot for every scene  and the numerous changes to the script  increased the shooting schedule from six  months to over a year as seen in the  behind the scenes documentary jack  nicholson reportedly gave up memorizing  the script due to the daily rewrites  instead he'd just do a quick study of  the scene prior to filming everybody's  got their script out you can look right  over and know if they've got the latest  version but of course you'll never have  the latest version  70 year old scotsman crothers also  suffered due to coo bricks perfectionism  according to the guardian kubrick  demanded 60 retakes for one scene which  made crothers cry in exasperation yet  none of these were as high as the shine  scene whose 148 retakes earned it the  guinness world record for most retakes  for one scene with dialogue however  there were some in the crew who didn't  mind all of the retakes in an interview  with the independent steadicam operator  garrett brown said i realized on day one  it was a priceless opportunity for me to  perfect what i was doing really to learn  the physical act of doing it i could  have literally done 100 takes or 1 000  takes  since its release king has expressed  criticisms of the film that boiled down  to his frustration over the various  changes from his original version after  praising the look of the film in an  interview with deadline king admitted  when it's opened a lot of the reviews  weren't very favorable and i was one of  those reviewers i kept my mouth shut at  the time but i didn't care for it much  among his problems with the film king  felt that in kubrick's hands the overall  story is darker and more heartless than  what he envisioned he summed it up as  such to tcm saying i always thought that  the real difference between my take on  it and stanley kubrick's take on it was  this in my novel the hotel burns in  kubrick's movie the hotel freezes it's  the difference between warmth and cold  as grueling as it was filming the  shining apparently didn't impact the  child actors as much as the adults in an  interview with the guardian danny lloyd  describes how kubrick did what he could  to protect him from the scary aspects of  the shining so he wouldn't be scarred  for life in fact kubrick was so  successful that lloyd didn't even know  he was in a horror film and not a drama  until years later but danny  unfortunately struggled to find success  after the film and quit acting when he  was 13 or 14. however lloyd wasn't the  only child actor of the shining to make  that decision lisa and louise burns  played the creepy and iconic grady twins  [Music]  come and play with us daddy  in spite of their success on screen they  decided to focus on academic pursuits  rather than careers and film years later  the pair appeared in an episode of vh1's  where are they now but they describes  their decidedly less creepy lives out of  the spotlight  twenty years after the release of his  novel king got his chance to redeem his  original story and tell it the way he  wanted to for television audiences the  shining miniseries was written and  produced by the horror master it's later  aired on abc in three parts in 1997.  featuring stephen weber as jack and  rebecca de mourney as wendy the small  screen adaptation shows the characters  in a way that is more true to how they  are written in the novel not only does  the mourner play a much stronger  character but it's also made clear that  webber's portrayal of jack truly  showcases the evil influence of the  undead the overlooked hotel is the main  cause of his descent into madness the tv  miniseries got a number of positive  reviews by critics and even won two emmy  awards for makeup and sound editing  one of the many hard to believe  conspiracy theories about the film's  secret messages is also one of the  darkest the theory is put forward in a  thorough analysis by film scholar rob  aeger in his book mazes mirrors  deception and denial and focuses on the  disturbing bear suit scene in the film a  man dressed in a bear costume performs a  sexual act on another man bears are a  recurring motif in kubrick's film and in  fact their image is associated with dany  several times in the movie aegin knows  that the use of this bear imagery  connects dany to the man in the bear  costume this frighteningly speaks to a  much darker and more sinister subtext  dany has been sexually abused by jack  agar points to other evidence of this  potential trauma he points out the  playgirl issue that jack is reading  which features a story with the title  incest why parents sleep with their  children there's also the similar use of  a mirror between dany's dream sequence  in room 237 where jack meets the rotting  old woman and the disturbing scene where  jack comforts dany while aegis  interpretation is his own even the  theory makes jack even more of a monster  than he already is  along with interpretations of the best  suit mystery there are many other  theories of hidden meanings throughout  the film kubrick was a well-known  perfectionist so when even a minor  detail is off many are quick to find  some logical reason for the discrepancy  but producer jan harlan is very clear  about his dismissal of the conspiracy  theory surrounding the film in an  interview with entertainment weekly he  says the most insulting one is the idea  that the shining is a film about the  holocaust that's outrageous that's an  insult to kubrick and also an insult to  victims of the holocaust the other ideas  are much more harmless where continuity  mistakes are attributed with deep  meaning it's difficult to argue with  harlan on this since according to the  independent kubrick actually did want to  make a film about the holocaust and  spent over 20 years developing a project  called the aryan papers which never came  to fruition  one condition that kubrick made warner  brothers agree to was that all the news  footage had to be destroyed as producer  jan harlan shared with entertainment  weekly stanley wanted to make sure that  nobody would ever reassemble his edit in  any other way the first of the unused  scenes show jack finding a scrapbook  that illustrates the dark events of the  overlooks past even though the scene did  not make the final cut the scrapbook can  still be seen on his desk for a moment  later in the film because it was so  important to the story as a result the  very end which shows the photograph of  jack from 1921 makes even less sense  without that cut scene it's good to see  you  it's good to be back lloyd kubrick also  took out an alternate ending because  warner brothers thought the movie was  too long after jack's famous demise in  the cold the scene shows wendy and dany  safe in a hospital the alternate ending  continues with the manager of the  overlook hotel telling them that an  investigation was done and no evidence  of paranormal activity has been found  however he follows that admittance by  handing dany the same tennis ball he  found outside the haunted room 237  ultimately leaving the audience  convinced of the supernatural events  they just witnessed  as if the filming of the shining could  not get any more chaotic the independent  reported that things took an even more  intense turn when a fire broke out at  elstree studios where the majority of  the film was shot luckily no one was  harmed but the flames caused all sorts  of damage kubrick not only seemed  unfazed but was even a bit happy about  the disaster in an interview with bbc  his widow kristiana kubrick says it gave  him a long delay which for some reason  he thought was quite fun because he  could rethink certain things raymarine  the sound editor also comments on how  the director only seems concerned with  making sure his audio recordings were  safe  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite horror masterpieces are  coming soon subscribe to our youtube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]

